A First-Principles Co-Design Framework for Robot-Native Photonic Communication Networks: A Theoretical Foundation

Ouadi Maakoul, Deepseek, ChatGPT, Meta, Grok XAi

---

Abstract

This dissertation establishes a rigorous theoretical framework for the co-design of robotic systems and their photonic communication substrates. Departing from the assumption that photonics is inherently superior to electronics, we construct the physical and mathematical conditions under which a robot-native photonic communication network provides a Pareto improvement over conventional electrical interconnects. The framework integrates: (i) a mixed-integer optimization model separating physical edge states from decision variables, with corrected three-index wavelength-continuity constraints; (ii) a functional safety formalism based on IEC 61508 and IEC 62061 with the black-channel principle of IEC 61784-3, including explicit FMEDA scope, architectural constraints, and a numerical residual-error budget; (iii) a validated coherent crosstalk penalty model for optical add-drop multiplexers using the amplitude-beating form with corrected N^2 coherent accumulation and a parallel Monte-Carlo statistical limit; (iv) a bounded finite-keyframe reconfiguration policy with a deterministic fallback topology proven to satisfy latency and SIL constraints for all keyframes; and (v) a three-level evaluation methodology grounded in Pareto dominance rather than scalar aggregation. The thesis does not assume superiority; it constructs the conditions under which superiority, equality, or inferiority can be demonstrated, and it identifies the experimental programme required to close the laboratory-to-robot gap.

