Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 Problem Statement

The integration of optical communication into robotic systems is established in prior art. The scientific question is not whether optical communication can be embedded in a robot, but rather:

\boxed{\text{How should a robot be architected if optical communication is a first-class physical substrate?}}

The central research question is formulated not in terms of preference but in terms of mathematical testability:

\boxed{\textit{Under what joint network, photonic, mechanical, thermal, and safety constraints does a robot-native photonic communication substrate provide a Pareto improvement over conventional electrical interconnects?}}

The term "preferable" is replaced by "Pareto improvement" because the latter is mathematically testable and does not introduce a priori bias.

1.2 Novelty Claim

The literature demonstrates optical interconnections within robotic arms and rotational interfaces. Commercial SIL 3-certified robot safety architectures using FSoE (Fail Safe over EtherCAT) are available. However, we have not identified, in the literature reviewed, a general architecture that simultaneously co-optimizes photonic topology, spectral/spatial allocation, thermo-mechanical constraints, routing, crosstalk, functional safety integrity, and dynamic reconfiguration as a unified constrained optimization problem. The contribution is the transition from link-level optical interconnects to network-level photonic substrate co-design.

1.3 Scope and Hypotheses

The framework is organized around two falsifiable hypotheses:

\boxed{H_A: \text{A hybrid electrical-safety / photonic-perception split reduces cable mass/volume by } > 30\% \text{ at equal SIL 3 compliance versus copper.}}

\boxed{H_B: \text{CWDM+SDM over POF/polymer achieves lower lifecycle cost than DWDM-on-silicon for joint-traversing links under vibration/thermal cycling.}}

All other considerations become supporting measurement.

