Chapter 2: Mathematical Framework

2.1 Physical Graph and Decision Variables

The robot is modeled as a directed graph G=(V,E). The physical state of an edge e \in E is:

e = (d, \alpha, \kappa, \epsilon, \Delta T, L_0, X_0, m, \rho, V_e)

where d is length, \alpha attenuation, \kappa curvature, \epsilon mechanical strain, \Delta T thermal excursion, L_0 intrinsic loss, X_0 characteristic crosstalk/isolation, m material, \rho linear mass density, and V_e occupied volume.

The decision variables are:

x_{f,e,\theta_k} \in \{0,1\}, \quad y_{f,\lambda} \in \{0,1\}, \quad z_{e,m} \in \{0,1\}, \quad q_{f,s} \in \{0,1\}, \quad s_e \in \{0,1\}

This separation transforms the model into a mixed-integer optimization problem rather than a vector mixing properties and decisions.

2.2 Flow Conservation and Wavelength Continuity

For each flow f and each keyframe \theta_k, flow conservation must hold:

\sum_{e \in \delta^+(v)} x_{f,e,\theta_k} - \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} x_{f,e,\theta_k} = 
\begin{cases}
1 & v = \text{src}(f) \\
-1 & v = \text{dst}(f) \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

Wavelength continuity requires a three-index variable x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k}:

\sum_{\lambda} y_{f,\lambda} = 1 \quad \forall f

x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \leq y_{f,\lambda} \quad \forall e \in \mathcal{P}_f, \forall \lambda, \forall \theta_k

\sum_{\lambda} x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} = x_{f,e,\theta_k} \quad \forall e, \forall \theta_k

This formulation ensures that if connection f is assigned wavelength \lambda, every link in the route uses the same wavelength.

2.3 Bend-Radius Constraints

For each edge e traversing a joint with minimum bend radius R_{\min}:

\kappa_e \leq \frac{1}{R_{\min}}

with R_{\min} = 25\,\text{mm} for standard POF, or R_{\min} = 1\,\text{mm} for paper-like flexible polymer waveguide films. Flexible silicone polymer waveguides have demonstrated flexing down to radii of 1.0 mm without cracking, with optical loss values less than 0.05 dB/cm at 850 nm .

2.4 Big-M Linearization with Wavelength Index

The product term in the crosstalk constraint is linearized via auxiliary variables w_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k}:

w_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \leq x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k}, \quad
w_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \leq y_{f,\lambda}, \quad
w_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \geq x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} + y_{f,\lambda} - 1

2.5 Coherent Crosstalk Penalty: Amplitude-Beating Model

For a single dominant crosstalk source with worst-case polarization alignment and phase, the Gaussian-approximation power penalty at \text{BER} = 10^{-9} (Q = 6) is:

\boxed{\text{PP}_{\text{homodyne}} \approx -10 \log_{10}\left(1 - 4 \epsilon Q^2 \frac{1+r}{(1-r)^2}\right)}

where \epsilon is the crosstalk-to-signal power ratio and r is the signal extinction ratio. For ideal extinction (r \to 0):

\text{PP}_{\text{homodyne}} \approx -10 \log_{10}(1 - 4 \epsilon Q^2)

The critical crosstalk is \epsilon_{\text{crit}} = 1/144 \approx -21.6\,\text{dB}.

2.6 Coherent Accumulation: Deterministic and Statistical Limits

For worst-case coherent homodyne crosstalk with N cascaded OADMs, field amplitudes add constructively:

\boxed{\epsilon_{\text{cum}} = N^2 \epsilon_{\text{stage}}}

Using \epsilon_{\text{stage}} = 10^{-3} and \text{PP}_{\max} = 1\,\text{dB}:

N_{\max} \approx \sqrt{\frac{1-0.794}{4 \times 36 \times 10^{-3}}} \approx 1.2

This deterministic N^2 bound is retained for safety-critical flows. In parallel, the statistical distribution of relative phases under realistic vibration spectra (T5) is quantified via Monte-Carlo simulation. Coherent and incoherent crosstalk together lead to a range of possible power penalties, and the number of nodes through which a signal is transmitted does not affect the power penalty distribution under statistical phase averaging . The dissertation presents both the deterministic N^2 limit and the statistical Monte-Carlo limit side-by-side: the architectural consequence of almost no cascading applies to safety-critical traffic, while non-safety traffic may exploit statistical averaging for higher cascade counts.

2.7 Multi-Objective Optimization

\min \mathcal{J} = \left[ X_{\text{cum}},\ L_{\text{tot}},\ E_{\text{bit}},\ \tau,\ M_{\text{masse}},\ V_{\text{volume}},\ R_{\text{failure}},\ C_{\text{lifecycle}} \right]

subject to BER, power, latency, crosstalk, reliability, strain, thermal, and homodyne penalty constraints for all f \in \mathcal{F} and all t \in T.

