Chapter 3: Safety Integrity and Black Channel Architecture

3.1 SIL/PFH Formalism

For IEC 61508 high-demand/continuous mode:

SIL PFH (per hour)
1 10^{-6} \leq \text{PFH} < 10^{-5}
2 10^{-7} \leq \text{PFH} < 10^{-6}
3 \boxed{10^{-8} \leq \text{PFH} < 10^{-7}}
4 10^{-9} \leq \text{PFH} < 10^{-8}

For a SIL 3 application in high-demand mode, the total PFH of the safety function must be below 10^{-7} per hour.

3.2 SIL as a Property of Safety Functions

SIL is attributed to a safety function S, not to a data packet:

\boxed{\operatorname{SIL}_{\text{achieved}}(S) \geq \operatorname{SIL}_{\text{required}}(S)}

The PFH sum \text{PFH}_S = \sum_i \text{PFH}_i is valid only for a series reliability structure with independent failures. If redundancy is used, a reliability block diagram or Markov model replaces the simple sum.

3.3 FMEDA and Architectural Constraints

For a SIL 3 claim at HFT = 1, either SFF \geq 90\% (Route 1H, Type A) or SFF \geq 99\% (Route 1H, Type B) is required. Optical switches, VCSELs, detectors and polymer connectors are more naturally Type B; claiming SIL 3 at HFT = 1 therefore typically requires SFF \geq 99\%.

3.4 Residual Error Budget: Numerical Allocation

IEC 61784-3 recommends that communication contribute less than one percent of the target SIL PFH. For SIL 3, the Residual Error Rate for the Safety Communication Layer is 10^{-9}/h . The numerical budget allocates concrete FIT contributions:

Impairment Allocation (FIT)
Homodyne floor under vibration ≤ 0.3
Connector micro-bend ≤ 0.3
Modal noise ≤ 0.2
Thermal drift of filters ≤ 0.2
Total SCL ≤ 1.0 FIT

The residual-error calculation is performed under the actual T5 environment, not under ideal laboratory bit-error conditions.

3.5 Black Channel Safety Mechanisms

The safety layer implements sequence numbering with time expectation, CRC for data integrity, connection authentication, watchdog timers, and diverse redundancy. The RER for authenticity errors may be assumed zero when connection-IDs are transmitted with every package . Timeliness and masquerade errors are bounded by the bitlengths of the T-code and A-code respectively .

3.6 Common-Cause Failure in the Switching Fabric

\text{PFH}_{\text{switch,CCF}} = \beta \cdot \lambda_{\text{switch}}

where \beta is the common-cause factor. For a switching fabric with M elements, the independent contribution is (1-\beta) \lambda_{\text{switch}}, and the common-cause contribution is \beta \lambda_{\text{switch}}.

3.7 Reliability vs. Safety Integrity

\boxed{\text{MTBF} \neq \text{safety integrity}}

Vibration PSD affects connectors and solder joints (dangerous undetected if intermittent). Thermal cycling affects lasers and polymer waveguides (dangerous detected via power monitor). Flexion cycles affect availability only (degradation detectable). Humidity affects connectors and polymers (dangerous undetected if corrosion-induced).

