Chapter 4: Architecture and Design

4.1 Hierarchical Topology

Level 0 (Electrical Local): Motors, servos, safety I/O, analog sensors. Safety loop closed locally.

Level 1 (Photonic Regional): Aggregation of sensor and actuator data within a robot segment.

Level 2 (Photonic Global): Redundant backbone connecting segments to the AI/NPU/memory core.

4.2 Axiom of Minimum Necessary Optical Traversal

\boxed{\min \sum_f w_f |\mathcal{P}_f|}

Justified by the deterministic N^2 coherent crosstalk limit. At -30\,\text{dB} per OADM and \text{PP}_{\max} = 1\,\text{dB}, cascading more than one OADM is not viable for worst-case homodyne crosstalk.

4.3 Finite Keyframe Policy with Proven Fallback

\Theta = \{\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_K\}, \quad K \leq 12 \text{ for 6-DOF arm}

The keyframe set is derived systematically from manipulability ellipsoid extrema, cable-strain extrema, and joint-limit corners. A deterministic fallback topology \mathcal{T}_{\text{fallback}} is defined such that for every keyframe and every safety function:

\boxed{\forall \theta \in \Theta, \forall S \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{safety}}: \exists \mathcal{P}_S \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{fallback}} \text{ s.t. } \tau_S \leq \tau_S^{\max} \text{ and } \operatorname{SIL}_S \geq \operatorname{SIL}_S^{\text{required}}}

Proof requirement: Exhaustive enumeration demonstrates that the fallback topology alone satisfies both the latency bound and the SIL residual-error budget for every keyframe, with zero reliance on reconfiguration.

4.4 SDM as Spatial Resource

\boxed{\text{SDM provides spatial resources for reconfiguration.}}

The reconfiguration function is a network-layer operation that may exploit SDM resources.

4.5 Reconfiguration Latency Distinction

Element settling time (< 100 \mus) is distinguished from system-level reconfiguration latency (1–10 ms). The fallback topology guarantees safety-critical flow availability without relying on reconfiguration.

