Chapter 6: Evaluation Methodology

6.1 Three-Level Evaluation

\boxed{\text{Feasible} \rightarrow \text{Pareto} \rightarrow \text{Lifecycle}}

Level 1 (Feasibility): Binary constraint satisfaction.

Level 2 (Pareto): Pareto front in \left[ B/M, B/V, 1/E_{\text{bit}}, -\tau, -\tau_{\text{reconfig}}, -X, -P_{\text{tuning}}, -R_f, -C_{\text{lifecycle}} \right].

Level 3 (Lifecycle): Monte Carlo with \geq 10^4 samples over MTTR and downtime cost.

6.2 Reliability Architecture

R_{\text{system}}(t) = \Phi(R_{\text{optical}}, R_{\text{electrical}}, R_{\text{mechanical}}, R_{\text{thermal}}, R_{\text{connector}})

\boxed{\text{MTBF} \neq \text{safety integrity}}

6.3 Reference Workload Suite

T₁: High-speed pick-and-place. T₂: Complex trajectory following. T₃: Multi-sensor fusion. T₄: Emergency stop (SIL 3). T₅: Thermal cycling and vibration.

6.4 Falsification Criteria

\boxed{\text{If POF link PER exceeds } 10^{-5} \text{ under T5 while copper remains compliant, } H_A \text{ is rejected for safety-relevant segments.}}

\boxed{\text{If system-level reconfiguration latency exceeds 1 ms for } > 10\% \text{ of keyframe transitions under T2, the finite policy approach must be redesigned.}}

\boxed{\text{If DWDM thermal tuning power exceeds 20\% of total link power under T5, CWDM+SDM superiority is supported for joint-traversing links, subject to full lifecycle cost analysis.}}

6.5 H_A Measurement Protocol

Same 6-DOF arm, same T1–T5 workloads, identical black-channel safety protocol, mass with calibrated scale (± 0.1 g), volume via water displacement or 3D scanning. For H_B, Monte Carlo sample size \geq 10^4, connector replacement interval from drag-chain cycle testing (> 50 million cycles).

