Chapter 7: Conclusion and Research Programme

This dissertation establishes a rigorous theoretical foundation for robot-native photonic communication networks:

1. Corrected SIL/PFH formalism distinguishing reliability from safety integrity, attributing SIL to safety functions, and adopting the correct PFH range for SIL 3 (10^{-8} to 10^{-7} per hour).
2. Validated coherent crosstalk model with the amplitude-beating power penalty and corrected deterministic N^2 accumulation, supplemented by a parallel Monte-Carlo statistical limit for non-safety traffic.
3. Mixed-integer optimization model with three-index wavelength-continuity constraints.
4. Finite-keyframe policy (K \leq 12) with a deterministic fallback topology proven to satisfy latency and SIL constraints for every keyframe.
5. Black channel principle with a numerical residual-error budget allocating ≤ 1 FIT to the Safety Communication Layer.
6. Common-cause failure treatment via \beta-factor model.
7. Three-level evaluation with falsification criteria stated in advance.

The decisive next action is experimental: build a minimal two- or three-joint hybrid demonstrator realising Level-0 electrical safety loops, photonic black-channel safety messages, and the finite-keyframe policy; run the identical T1–T5 suite on a copper twin and the photonic hybrid; publish the measured Pareto surface before any numerical superiority claim is retained; and engage a functional-safety assessor on the residual-error-rate calculations while the hardware is still being designed. Closing the laboratory-to-robot gap with quantitative side-by-side data will convert this theoretical foundation into an actionable co-design methodology.

