Appendix B: Mixed-Integer Linear Programming Formulation — Complete Details

B.1 Full Constraint Set

The complete MILP formulation is:

\min \mathcal{J} = \left[ X_{\text{cum}},\ L_{\text{tot}},\ E_{\text{bit}},\ \tau,\ M_{\text{masse}},\ V_{\text{volume}},\ R_{\text{failure}},\ C_{\text{lifecycle}} \right]

subject to the following constraints for all f \in \mathcal{F}, all e \in E, all \lambda \in \Lambda, and all \theta_k \in \Theta.

Flow conservation (C1):

\sum_{e \in \delta^+(v)} x_{f,e,\theta_k} - \sum_{e \in \delta^-(v)} x_{f,e,\theta_k} = 
\begin{cases}
1 & v = \text{src}(f) \\
-1 & v = \text{dst}(f) \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

Wavelength uniqueness per flow (C2):

\sum_{\lambda} y_{f,\lambda} = 1 \quad \forall f

Link-wavelength consistency (C3):

x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \leq y_{f,\lambda} \quad \forall e \in \mathcal{P}_f, \forall \lambda, \forall \theta_k

Aggregation to routing variables (C4):

\sum_{\lambda} x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} = x_{f,e,\theta_k} \quad \forall e, \forall \theta_k

Bend-radius constraint (C5):

\kappa_e \leq \frac{1}{R_{\min}} \quad \forall e

Big-M linearization (C6):

w_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \leq x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k}

w_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \leq y_{f,\lambda}

w_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \geq x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} + y_{f,\lambda} - 1

Crosstalk constraint (C7):

\sum_{f} \sum_{e} \sum_{\lambda} \sum_{\theta_k} w_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \cdot X_0 \leq X_{\max}

Latency constraint (C8):

\sum_{e \in \mathcal{P}_f} \tau_e \leq \tau_f^{\max} \quad \forall f

Power budget constraint (C9):

P_{\text{laser}} + P_{\text{TEC}} + P_{\text{driver}} + P_{\text{tuning}} \leq P_{\text{budget}}

BER constraint (C10):

\text{BER}_f(t) \leq \text{BER}_f^{\max} \quad \forall f, \forall t

Homodyne penalty constraint (C11):

\text{PP}_{\text{homodyne}} \leq \text{PP}_{\max}

B.2 Big-M Linearization: Correctness Proof

The product x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \cdot y_{f,\lambda} is nonlinear. The auxiliary variable w_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} satisfies:

· If x = 0 or y = 0: constraints w \leq x and w \leq y force w = 0.
· If x = 1 and y = 1: constraint w \geq x + y - 1 = 1 forces w = 1.

Therefore w = x \cdot y exactly, without approximation.

B.3 Wavelength Continuity: Structural Correctness

The three-index formulation ensures that if flow f is assigned wavelength \lambda, then every edge in its path uses the same wavelength. The constraint x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} \leq y_{f,\lambda} ensures that an edge can only be used at wavelength \lambda if that wavelength is assigned to the flow. The aggregation constraint \sum_{\lambda} x_{f,e,\lambda,\theta_k} = x_{f,e,\theta_k} ensures that exactly one wavelength is selected per edge traversal. The wavelength uniqueness constraint \sum_{\lambda} y_{f,\lambda} = 1 ensures that each flow uses exactly one wavelength.

