Appendix F: VCSEL and Polymer Waveguide Reliability Data

F.1 VCSEL Random Failure Rate

850 nm VCSELs have field experience of over 100 million units deployed in data centre applications, with a random failure rate lower than 1 FIT. Internal random failure rate tests accumulated approximately 0.3 billion device hours from over 80,000 chips from more than 2,000 wafers. The failure criterion was >1 dB power drop. FIT rates are limited by accumulated device hours, not by failures, and further decrease over time is expected. Test results show < 1 FIT at 40°C and < 7 FIT at 105°C substrate temperature, satisfying the automotive requirement of < 10 FIT.

F.2 VCSEL Wear-Out Lifetime

Wear-out failure mode is not observed in the field. Accelerated lifetime tests at 9 mA and 125°C show a conservative MFT > 9,000 hours. The release criteria for datacom is TT1%F > 10 years at 70°C/8 mA. The TT1%F at different operating conditions is calculated by an aging model with E_a = 1.3 eV. At 7 mA and 70°C ambient, 1 ppm cumulated failures occurs after 28.8 years.

F.3 Polymer Waveguide Reliability

Flexible silicone polymer waveguides based on Dow Corning WG-1017 clad and WG-1010 core exhibit:

· Mechanical flexibility permitting waveguide flexing down to radii of 1.0 mm without cracking
· Stability in damp heat: 2000 h in 85°C/85% RH with no degradation in optical performance
· Thermal shock: 500 cycles from −40°C to +120°C
· Lead-free solder reflow up to 260°C
· Optical loss less than 0.05 dB/cm at 850 nm

These results are material- and process-specific and must be treated as hypotheses pending measurement on the exact film used in the robot demonstrator.

