Appendix G: Keyframe Selection Algorithm

G.1 Manipulability Ellipsoid

The manipulability ellipsoid at configuration \boldsymbol{q} is defined by the matrix:

\boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q}) = \boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q}) \boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}

where \boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q}) is the Jacobian of the manipulator. The eigenvalues \lambda_i of \boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q}) determine the radii of the ellipsoid. The manipulability measure is:

w(\boldsymbol{q}) = \sqrt{\det(\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q}) \boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top})}

Configurations with low manipulability (near singularities) produce extreme cable strains and are candidates for keyframe selection.

G.2 Keyframe Selection Algorithm

Algorithm 1: Systematic Keyframe Selection

Input: Robot kinematic model, joint limits \boldsymbol{q}_{\min}, \boldsymbol{q}_{\max}, cable routing topology.

Output: Keyframe set \Theta = \{\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_K\}.

1. Manipulability extrema: Sample the configuration space on a uniform grid. For each joint j, identify configurations that maximise and minimise the manipulability index w(\boldsymbol{q}).
2. Cable-strain extrema: For each cable path traversing joints, compute the strain \epsilon_e(\boldsymbol{q}) as a function of configuration. Identify configurations that maximise \epsilon_e.
3. Joint-limit corners: Include configurations at the corners of the joint-limit hyperrectangle: \boldsymbol{q} = (q_1^{\min/\max}, \ldots, q_n^{\min/\max}).
4. Clustering: Cluster the candidate configurations using k-means in joint space with k = K. The cluster centroids form the initial keyframe set.
5. Hysteresis assignment: For each keyframe \theta_k, define a hysteresis region \Delta \theta such that the network state is held constant until the configuration exits the region, preventing chattering.
6. Validation: Verify that the fallback topology \mathcal{T}_{\text{fallback}} satisfies latency and SIL constraints for every keyframe in \Theta.

G.3 Bound on Keyframe Count

For a 6-DOF arm, the number of candidate extrema is:

· Manipulability extrema: 2 \times 6 = 12 configurations
· Cable-strain extrema: 2 \times n_{\text{cables}} configurations
· Joint-limit corners: 2^6 = 64 configurations

After clustering with K = 12, the keyframe set has at most 12 representative poses. This bound is adopted as a starting hypothesis and is to be validated systematically for the specific kinematic chain.

