Appendix H: Monte Carlo Lifecycle Cost Parameters

H.1 Cost Model

The lifecycle cost is:

C_{\text{lifecycle}} = C_{\text{capital}} + C_{\text{maintenance}} + C_{\text{downtime}} + C_{\text{energy}} - C_{\text{residual}}

where:

· C_{\text{capital}}: Initial procurement and installation cost
· C_{\text{maintenance}}: Scheduled and corrective maintenance cost
· C_{\text{downtime}}: Production loss due to unavailability
· C_{\text{energy}}: Energy consumption over lifecycle
· C_{\text{residual}}: Residual value at end of life

H.2 Downtime Cost

C_{\text{downtime}} = \sum_{i} \text{MTTR}_i \cdot \text{Frequency}_i \cdot C_{\text{hour}}

where C_{\text{hour}} is the production loss per hour of downtime. Industrial robot production line estimates range from $10,000 to $100,000 per hour.

H.3 Monte Carlo Sampling

For each subsystem i:

· \text{MTBF}_i \sim \text{Exponential}(\lambda_i)
· \text{MTTR}_i \sim \text{LogNormal}(\mu_i, \sigma_i)

The number of samples is N_{\text{MC}} \geq 10^4. The output is the distribution of C_{\text{lifecycle}}, from which the mean, variance, and percentiles are computed.

H.4 Connector Replacement Interval

Drag-chain cycle testing of commercial cables shows minimum 25 million cycles for multi-pair cables and 10 million cycles for torsion-resistant cables. The connector replacement interval is derived from these accelerated-life data rather than generic exponential assumptions.

